To trace all system calls generated by the command cat sample.txt, use the strace utility in the Linux terminal
First, create a file named sample.txt and add some content to it.
Then execute the command strace cat sample.txt. The strace utility displays the system calls made by the cat command while reading and displaying the file.
Important system calls include execve() for executing the program, openat() for opening the file, read() for reading the file contents, write() for displaying the contents on the terminal, close() for closing the file, and exit_group() for terminating the process.
Thus, strace helps us understand how a user-level command communicates with the Linux kernel through system calls.
When the command cat sample.txt is executed, the cat program communicates with the Linux kernel using system calls. The main sequence is:
execve() – Loads and executes the cat program.
Kernel service: Process and program execution.
openat() – Opens sample.txt in read-only mode.
Kernel service: File and file-descriptor management. 
read() – Reads the contents of sample.txt into memory.
Kernel service: File input and data transfer.
write() – Sends the read contents to the terminal (standard output).
Kernel service: Output/I/O management.
 close() – Closes the file after reading is completed.
Kernel service: Resource and file-descriptor management.
exit_group() – Terminates the cat process.
Kernel service: Process termination and resource cleanup.
Thus, strace demonstrates that a simple command like cat sample.txt depends on several kernel services for process execution, file access, input/output, resource management, and process termination.
